// across Belgrade, Novi Sad, Niš, Kragujevac, Subotica and Bor.
// FIRST WESTERN BALKANS COUNTRY in coverage.
//
// LEGAL POSITIONING NOTE — READ BEFORE EDITING:
// Serbia is a RIGHT TIER market with an ALL-SUBJECT ANNUAL EXAM —
// closest to SLOVENIA, and more permissive on paper than most of
// Central Europe. Two features are genuinely distinctive.
// 1. THE RIGHT IS EXPLICIT, AND IT NAMES DISTANCE EDUCATION.
//    Under the Law on Primary Education and Upbringing (Zakon o
//    osnovnom obrazovanju i vaspitanju), Art. 8, a parent or legal
//    representative has the RIGHT to choose, for their child,
//    primary education in a public or private school, OR EDUCATION
//    AT HOME OR AT A DISTANCE ("obrazovanje kod kuće ili na
//    daljinu"). Very few education acts in Europe name distance
//    education as a parental choice in the same breath as school.
//    IMPORTANT HEDGE: this is favourable CONTEXT, not a blanket
//    authorisation of any foreign online provider. The minister
//    prescribes the closer conditions for realisation and for
//    quality assurance and evaluation of teaching at home and
//    distance teaching. Whether a given international programme
//    fits those conditions is confirmed with the enrolling school
//    and the Ministry — never asserted by us.
// 2. THE OBLIGATIONS (Art. 38), WHICH MUST ALWAYS APPEAR:
//    * The parent bears the COSTS of the education.
//    * The parent must notify the enrolling school IN WRITING, BY
//      THE END OF THE TEACHING YEAR, of the intention to organise
//      teaching at home from the FOLLOWING school year.
//    * The school is OBLIGED to organise RAZREDNI ISPITI — class
//      examinations — IN ALL SUBJECTS, in accordance with the
//      national plan and programme. Examinations are taken before
//      a three-member commission with at least two subject
//      specialists, formed by the school principal.
//    * Separately and distinctly: for pupils who cannot attend for
//      more than three weeks because of major health problems or
//      chronic illness, teaching is organised at home or in a
//      health institution, with the Ministry's consent. State that
//      factually; it is a welfare measure, not a pathway.
// 3. SECONDARY: the Law on Secondary Education and Upbringing ALSO
//    provides for acquiring secondary education at home and through
//    distance teaching, with the school keeping records, and again
//    with ministerial conditions. So Serbia does NOT have a simple
//    "post-15 free window" like Croatia or Hungary — the secondary
//    phase has its own named provision inside the system. Do NOT
//    market an unregulated post-compulsory window here. State that
//    primary education is compulsory, and that secondary home and
//    distance education exists as a provided-for route whose terms
//    are set by the school and the Ministry.
// CONSEQUENCE FOR POSITIONING: supplementary beside an enrolment is
// the clean default; for families exercising the right, Smartious is
// the ACADEMIC SPINE while the school runs the all-subject class
// examinations. Serbian-language and national-curriculum coverage
// must be planned all year (the Slovenia/Slovakia discipline).
// MARKET NOTE: the international tier is a Belgrade story —
// International School of Belgrade (ISB), Chartwell, Crnjanski, the
// British International School, Prima, Ruđer Bošković and peers,
// plus the French and Russian schools. Novi Sad has a little; Niš,
// Kragujevac, Subotica and the east have essentially none. Economy:
// a large and fast-growing IT and outsourcing sector (Belgrade, Novi
// Sad, Niš), the Stellantis plant at Kragujevac, Zijin's copper
// operations at Bor with a substantial Chinese workforce, NIS/energy,
// agriculture in Vojvodina, and a very large diaspora (Austria,
// Germany, Switzerland, USA, Canada, Australia).
// TIMEZONE: CET (UTC+1) / CEST (UTC+2 summer) — 1-2 hours behind
// Nairobi EAT, the standard European framing.
